<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/7th-century-bce">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Campaign</p><h1 class="enc-title">Assyrian conquest of Egypt</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">671 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century-bce">7th century BCE</a> · Also known as Esarhaddon's conquest of Egypt</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Assyrian conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> was the invasion of Egypt by the Assyrian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/esarhaddon">Esarhaddon</a>, who captured Memphis in 671 BCE and drove out the Kushite <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taharqa">Taharqa</a>. Assyrian control was later reasserted by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashurbanipal">Ashurbanipal</a>. The conquest marked the greatest territorial extent of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyrian Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/assyrian-conquest-of-egypt.jpg" alt="Assyrian conquest of Egypt" width="320" height="220" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nigyou · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Neo-Assyrian_map_824-671_BC.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taharqa">Taharqa</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a> of the Twenty-fifth Dynasty and ruler of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kushite-empire">Kingdom of Kush</a>, had long encouraged unrest inside the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neo-assyrian-empire">Neo-Assyrian Empire</a>, and for years <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> sponsored rebels against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a>. In 701 BCE Hezekiah of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judah">Judah</a> and the kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sidon">Sidon</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashkelon">Ashkelon</a> and Ekron allied with Egypt against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sennacherib">Sennacherib</a>, who defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egyptians">Egyptians</a> and drove them from the region. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/esarhaddon">Esarhaddon</a> hoped to remove this rival in one stroke, but an earlier invasion, made at great speed, ended in defeat by Taharqa's forces outside Ashkelon, after which he withdrew to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nineveh">Nineveh</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Early in 671 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/esarhaddon">Esarhaddon</a> marched again with a much larger army, more slowly. At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harran">Harran</a> a prophecy foretold his success, and three months later his forces won their first battle with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egyptians">Egyptians</a>; eleven days afterwards he performed the substitute king ritual, which left him unable to command in person. Memphis was taken and sacked, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taharqa">Taharqa</a> fled south, and his wife, his son Ushankhuru and most of the court were sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a> as hostages, with many other Egyptians. Governors loyal to Assyria were set over the north, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/necho-i">Necho I</a> was made king at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sais">Sais</a>.</p><p>In 669 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taharqa">Taharqa</a> reoccupied Memphis and the Delta, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/esarhaddon">Esarhaddon</a> died on a new campaign. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashurbanipal">Ashurbanipal</a> defeated Taharqa in 667 and marched as far as Thebes, then made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/necho-i">Necho I</a> and Necho's son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/psamtik-i">Psamtik I</a> his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a>. After Taharqa's death in 664, his successor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tantamani">Tantamani</a> retook <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and Necho I was killed. In 663 Ashurbanipal returned, and with Psamtik I's Carian mercenaries defeated Tantamani near Memphis. Assyrian annals record that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes">Thebes</a> was heavily plundered and its people deported, though archaeology shows more continuity than destruction there.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The conquest brought a land of great cultural prestige under Assyrian rule and carried the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neo-assyrian-empire">Neo-Assyrian Empire</a> to the widest reach it ever had. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/esarhaddon">Esarhaddon</a> set up stelae at Nahr el-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banu-kalb">Kalb</a> and at Zincirli Höyük, and three colossal statues of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taharqa">Taharqa</a> found at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nineveh">Nineveh</a> were probably war trophies. The reconquest ended Nubian control of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>: in 656 BCE Psamtik I's navy took Thebes peacefully, uniting the country under the Twenty-sixth Dynasty. Psamtik threw off Assyrian ties about 655 BCE, yet Egypt stayed friendly, and in 609 BCE Necho II fought beside the last <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyrians</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harran">Harran</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Assyrian conquest of Egypt” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The empire's greatest phase was the Neo-Assyrian revival, which began in 911 BCE under Adad-nirari II and culminated in <b>Esarhaddon's conquest of Egypt</b> in 671 BCE, when Assyria became the largest empire the world had yet seen.</blockquote><a href="/empire/assyrian-empire">Assyrian Empire · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
